<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis I, Duke of Nevers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1516">1516</a>–<a href="/years/1561">1561</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as François de Clèves, François I de Clèves · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-marck">House of La Marck</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis I, Duke of Nevers, born François de Clèves, was a French nobleman and soldier of the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, which had held the county of Nevers since 1491. In 1539 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a> raised Nevers to a duchy-peerage for him, making him its first duke. He served Francis I and Henry II in their wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, and through his daughter Henriette the duchy later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">Gonzaga family</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers.jpg" alt="Francis I, Duke of Nevers" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of François Clouet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francis_I_of_Cleves,_duke_of_Nevers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>François de Clèves was born on 2 September 1516, the only son of Charles II of Nevers, who died in 1521, and Marie d'Albret, Countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethel">Rethel</a>, and he succeeded his father as Count of Nevers and Eu. He ranked as a prince étranger at the French court. In 1538 he married Marguerite of Bourbon-Vendôme, daughter of Charles de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, Duke of Vendôme, receiving 100,000 livres of dowry and a royal gift of 20,000 more. Their children were tied by marriage to the leading, largely Protestant, princely families of the northern frontier, the La Marck and the Croÿ.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1545 he was named governor of Champagne, where he held much land, and on his mother's death in 1549 he inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethel">Rethel</a>, Beaufort and other lordships there; his titles were then worth at least 2 million livres. He spent little time in his province, being taken up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> under Henri II. He fought in the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> in 1552, and in 1554 he led one of the three royal armies that devastated Artois. That July he was with the small force of about 2,000 under François, Duke of Guise, that defeated the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> at Renty.</p><p>After the disaster at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-quentin">Saint-Quentin</a> he was among the commanders who led the remnants of the French army to safety, and he helped in the successful defence of Thionville. In 1557 Protestant pastors counted him among the nobles who might support the worshippers arrested after the rue Saint-Jacques affair. He jousted with the king at the celebrations of the peace that ended the wars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As Protestants grew bolder in Champagne, he banned Catholic processions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a> as unsafe after an attack on a statue of the Virgin Mary. In 1560 he married again, in a double wedding at Saint-Germain-en-Laye. On his deathbed he was allowed to resign the governorship directly to his son, a rare privilege, and he died on 13 February 1561. His campaigns had consumed much of his fortune, and his son was soon forced into bankruptcy. Both his sons died within a few years, and the duchy passed through his daughter Henriette to Louis Gonzaga.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “François I, Duke of Nevers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Imperial forces drove out the French, and the Battle of Pavia in 1525, fought near the duchy’s second city, ended with <b>Francis I</b> a prisoner of Charles V.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Jacques Cartier reached the Gulf of St. Lawrence in 1534, claiming territory for <b>Francis I</b>, but decades of religious civil war prevented meaningful colonisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>France’s claim in North America began in 1534, when Jacques Cartier, sailing for <b>Francis I</b>, explored the Gulf of St Lawrence and planted a cross on the Gaspé Peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The long Ottoman-Habsburg rivalry structured European geopolitics from the 1520s to the late seventeenth century, and the Ottoman factor repeatedly affected European alliances, including the Franco-Ottoman alliance concluded under <b>Francis I</b> and Suleiman.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1491 it passed to the house of Cleves, and in 1539 <b>Francis I</b> raised it to a duchy-peerage for François de Clèves.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nevers">County of Nevers</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Nevers, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.99,3.16&amp;year=1545" aria-label="Nevers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Francis I, Duke of Nevers"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5184 6099h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5184 6099h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=46.99,3.16&amp;year=1545">What was here in 1545? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
